00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:55.710
her talk to please submit your questions via
the little question submission mechanism here

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.729
on the right side-- usually on the right side
of your display.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:05.210
And we'll gather all those up and ask them
at the end of Dr. Witten's presentation.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:10.179
So without further ado, Dr. Witten, thank
you very much for presenting to us today.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.480
Thanks so much for the introduction.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.560
And so today, as we just heard, I'm going
to talk about supervised machine learning.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.140
And just to give you an overview, machine
learning basically falls into two camps--

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.330
supervised learning and also unsupervised
learning.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:33.890
So the idea behind supervised learning is
that we use a data set, x, to predict or to

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.690
detect an association with a response, y.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:44.510
So examples of supervised learning include
regression where we're trying to predict a

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:50.910
quantitative response, classification where
we're trying to predict a categorical response.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.421
And you're going to hear a lot about supervised
learning today from me.

00:02:53.421 --> 00:02:54.421
But I do briefly just want to mention that
the other big type of machine learning is

00:02:54.421 --> 00:02:55.421
learning today from me.

00:02:55.421 --> 00:02:59.500
But I do briefly just want to mention that
the other big type of machine learning is

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.510
unsupervised learning.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:06.300
And the idea behind unsupervised learning
is that we want to discover the signal in

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.660
a matrix, X, and no y is available.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.300
So unsupervised learning is an incredibly
important topic.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.940
And that's actually going to be covered in
next week's webinar.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:24.680
And today instead, I will just be focusing
on supervised learning.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.050
So there's a couple different flavors of supervised
learning.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.170
And maybe the best known flavor is regression.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:37.569
So the idea behind regression is that you
want to predict a quantitative response such

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:41.950
as blood pressure, cholesterol level, tumor
size.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:47.209
So the thing that makes regression regression
is that the response, which I'm going to call

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:52.620
y today, is quantitative.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.680
So the other main flavor of supervised learning
is classification.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.920
And in classification, we are trying to predict
a categorical response such as tumor versus

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:11.580
normal tissue, heart disease versus no heart
disease, subtype of disease, and so on.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.350
So the only difference between regression
and classification is the type of your response,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.350
y.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:20.769
If it's a quantitative response, you're in
the regression framework.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:28.580
If it's a categorical or qualitative response,
you're in the classification framework.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:34.439
And one thing that I really want to emphasize
is that it doesn't matter what type of data

00:04:34.439 --> 00:04:36.270
matrix, X, you have.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.490
So as I'll mention in a couple minutes, the
idea is you have a data matrix, X, which contains

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.409
measurements for your variables or your features.

00:04:43.409 --> 00:04:47.020
You're trying to predict this response, y.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:53.059
So I don't care if your data matrix, X, is
quantitative, if it's categorical.

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:54.379
It doesn't matter.

00:04:54.379 --> 00:04:57.939
It's still going to be regression if your
response, y, is quantitative.

00:04:57.939 --> 00:05:03.529
And it'll still be classification if your
response, y, is categorical.

00:05:03.529 --> 00:05:08.960
So regression and classification are the two
main flavors of supervised learning.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.129
There are also other flavors like if you have
a response that's a survival time, which is

00:05:14.129 --> 00:05:20.409
possibly censored because some people might
have died, or dropped out of the study, and

00:05:20.409 --> 00:05:26.050
so on, then that is a slightly different flavor
of supervised learning.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:31.889
But the main ideas that apply to regression
also hold for these other flavors of supervised

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:33.539
learning.

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:36.099
And so today, I'm going to just be talking
about regression.

00:05:36.099 --> 00:05:45.729
But again, all of these ideas extend directly
to other types of quantitative responses.

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:52.479
So I'm going to start off talking about linear
models, because linear models give us an incredibly

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:56.779
flexible framework for doing regression.

00:05:56.779 --> 00:06:01.740
And basically, if you're doing supervised
machine learning, 90% of what you need fits

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.779
within the linear model framework.

00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:08.800
And then at the end of my talk, I'm going
to just speak for a few minutes about a really

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:15.979
different type of supervised learning, which
is a decision tree approach.

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:20.210
So to begin, we're in the linear model setting.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.279
And the idea is that we have n observations.

00:06:24.279 --> 00:06:28.719
And for the ith observation, I'm going to
call the response y sub i.

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:36.029
And I'm going to let xi1 through xiP denote
the P features for that ith observation.

00:06:36.029 --> 00:06:41.779
So for example, yi might be blood pressure,
which is a quantitative value.

00:06:41.779 --> 00:06:45.160
And then xi1 through xiP are a bunch of features.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.419
Maybe these are biomarkers that we think are
predictive of blood pressure.

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:52.129
So we want to use these features to predict
the response.

00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:56.429
And in a linear model, we do this as follows.

00:06:56.429 --> 00:07:02.119
We say that yi is just equal to intercept
beta naught plus the coefficient beta 1, which

00:07:02.119 --> 00:07:08.479
gets multiplied by the first feature, xi1
and so on all the way through to the Pth feature,

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:12.460
where beta P is multiplied by xiP plus a noise
term, epsilon.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:20.939
So epsilon sub i is the noise term associated
with the ith observation.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:25.460
And the idea is that we need to estimate these
unknown coefficients, beta naught through

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:30.629
beta P. In other words, we have to fit the
model.

00:07:30.629 --> 00:07:35.370
So actually there's a lot of ways we could
fit this model.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.029
And I'm going to start by talking about the
best known way to fit this model.

00:07:39.029 --> 00:07:43.749
And then I'll talk about the fact that that
best known way isn't necessarily the best

00:07:43.749 --> 00:07:47.439
approach, especially in the context of biomedical
big data.

00:07:47.439 --> 00:07:52.759
But first, let's talk about the simplest way
to fit a linear model.

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:58.270
And if you took STAT 101, then you probably
saw the least squares approach for fitting

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.219
a linear model.

00:08:00.219 --> 00:08:04.900
And the idea behind least squares is that
we look for estimates of the coefficients.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:09.009
If you see a little hat on a coefficient,
that means it's an estimate.

00:08:09.009 --> 00:08:14.289
So I'm looking for a beta naught hat, beta
1 hat, all the way through to beta P hat such

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:18.669
that the sum of squared errors is as small
as possible.

00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:26.419
So I'm going to sum the quantity, yi, minus
the prediction or the fitted value for my

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:27.419
model.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:31.189
I get that by plugging in the coefficient
estimates.

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:33.320
And then I'm going to square that quantity.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.099
So this entire term here is called a sum of
squared errors.

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:40.100
It's also called a residual sum of squares.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.820
And when I fit a model using these squares,
I'm just looking for these coefficient estimates

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.470
such that this residual sum of squares is
as small as possible.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.900
A simpler way to represent this is to say
that I'm just looking for coefficient estimates

00:08:53.900 --> 00:09:01.340
such that the sum of yi minus yi hat squared
is as small as possible where yi hat is the

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.260
prediction from my model.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:08.120
Or you can also call it the fitted value from
the model.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:15.430
So this is a really natural way to fit a linear
model.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:20.890
And it works pretty well, especially in the
old fashioned data setting where you have

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:27.070
three, or five, or eight variables that you
measured and you have a huge number of observations.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.720
So this works really well if n is large and
P is really small.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:30.720
But as we'll see in a few minutes, often in
observations.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.720
So this works really well if n is large and
P is really small.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:35.950
But as we'll see in a few minutes, often in
the context of biomedical big data, least

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.090
squares is not the best way to go.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:43.780
But before I get there, here's a picture showing
what least squares is actually doing.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:49.560
So here is an example with the response, y,
and two predictors, x1 and x2.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.670
So the observations are shown as red circles.

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:56.450
And the least squares fit to the data is a
plane.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.990
It's that green-blue plane that you see in
the middle here.

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:03.110
And the reason that this is a plane is because
we have two features.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:07.800
So instead of a least squares line, we have
a least squares plane.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:14.810
And these black vertical bars here, they tell
us how far yi is from the prediction for yi

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.250
with our model.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:22.660
So in other words, this black vertical bar
tells me the distance, yi minus yi hat.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:29.060
And so this blue least squares plane is actually
exactly positioned such that the sum of yi

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:31.320
minus yi hat squared is as small as possible.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.240
In other words, so that the sum of squared
black lines is as small as possible.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.320
And you can sort of imagine that if you shifted
the plane up or down, or if you wiggled it

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:47.700
a little bit, then the sum of squared black
lines would actually increase.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.570
OK.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:54.060
You've probably seen least squares regression
before.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.730
This is something that comes up like in a
classical statistics class.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:00.880
But I want to talk now a little bit about
the machine learning perspective on what we're

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.280
doing.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:06.280
So in machine learning when we talk about
fitting a model, we say that we're fitting

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.300
a model on a training set.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:14.430
So the training set refers to the data that
you use to actually fit your model.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:20.841
And given that we fit a model on the training
set, we can think about the error of our model

00:11:20.841 --> 00:11:22.170
on the training set.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.890
Usually we think about the mean squared error.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.330
And you've actually seen this before.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:32.960
This is just one over n times that residual
sum of squares quantity that we saw a couple

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.650
of slides ago.

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:38.721
So remember the way that we chose the coefficients,
beta naught hat, beta 1 hat through beta P

00:11:38.721 --> 00:11:43.380
hat was actually just to make this quantity
as small as possible.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.480
So we basically are guaranteed that the mean
squared error is going to be a decently small

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:52.340
on the training set, because we literally
chose these coefficient estimates in order

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.860
to make this mean squared error small.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:59.000
But what I'm trying to emphasize here is that
this mean squared error, right now what I'm

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.120
showing you is the mean squared error calculated
on the training set.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:06.430
And moving forward, I'm going to call this
the training error.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:11.280
So this mean squared error, it gives me a
representation of how good my model is on

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.000
the data that I use to fit my model.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.220
It does not give me an idea of how good my
model would be hypothetically on data that

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:21.900
I did not use to fit my model.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:27.940
So if you're familiar with R squared from
a linear model, an R squared is just really

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:30.530
closely related to the training error.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:33.070
When R squared is big, your training error
is small.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:38.330
There's really actually a one to one mapping
between those two quantities.

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:45.560
So it turns out that training error, and equivalently
R squared, are not a great way to think about

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.370
how good your model is.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.180
And the reason that they're not good is because
they will always improve as we fit a more

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:53.720
complex model.

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:59.380
So like imagine that first you fit a model
with three variables and you calculate your

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:04.960
training error and then you say, hey actually
maybe I should just add in one more variable

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:06.640
to my model.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.770
Well your training error is going to go down.

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:18.410
It's literally guaranteed to go down or maybe
stay the same as you increase the number of

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:20.030
variables.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.830
So training error, we can see right away,
there's kind of a problem, because it's going

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:29.290
to look better and better and better as we
add more and more variables to our model.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:35.010
And the problem basically is that training
error quantifies the fit to the training data.

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:39.540
So as you fit a more and more complex model
to the training data, your model is going

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.440
to look better and better on the training
data-- actually to the point where if you

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:51.570
fit a complex enough model, we can get a perfect
fit to the training data with 0 error.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:55.710
But the problem here is that what we really
care about is the model's performance not

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:58.990
on the training data, but rather on a test
set.

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:04.900
So for me, a model is good not if it accurately
describes the training data, but actually

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:10.380
if it accurately describes the test set, because
I don't really care about predicting the response

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:15.270
on the data that I already collected, because
I know the response on the data that I already

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.270
collected.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:20.220
What I care about is predicting the response
on a new observation for which the response

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:24.690
is unknown that was not part of my training
set.

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:30.980
So if you're coming up with some way to predict
survival time on the basis of gene expression

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:36.550
among patients with some disease, I already
know the survival time for the patients that

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.190
I'm using to fit my model.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:43.180
So doing a really great job predicting the
survival time for the patients who are in

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:45.670
my training set is really not useful.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:50.320
But what I need to do is do a really good
job predicting survival time for a new patient

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:54.240
who walks into my clinic tomorrow or next
week.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.150
And that's really the idea behind the fact
that the training error is getting at the

00:14:58.150 --> 00:14:59.360
wrong quantity.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:04.250
Instead, I need to be getting at the model's
performance on a test set.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:11.460
So just to reiterate, as we fit a more complex
model-- in particular, as we increase the

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:16.820
number of variables-- the R
squared of our linear model will always increase

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:20.300
and the training error will always decrease.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:25.010
So that's just really important to realize,
because it gives us an idea of the fact that

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:31.060
training error and R squared are telling us
the wrong thing.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:36.210
So as I mentioned, what we care about is the
performance of the model on observations not

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.300
used to fit the model.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:43.529
And to quantify this a little bit more, what
I really care about is the difference between

00:15:43.529 --> 00:15:49.370
the response for a test observation and the
prediction for a test observation where here,

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:55.350
y hat test is the predicted value or the fitted
value that I'm going to get if I get a new

00:15:55.350 --> 00:16:01.180
test observation and I plug the variables
for that test observation into the model that

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:05.280
I fit on the training set.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:11.600
So the test error is the average of y test
minus y hat test squared over a bunch of test

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.089
observations.

00:16:13.089 --> 00:16:22.310
And what I really want is a model for which
the test error is small.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:26.589
And so we already saw the first two panels
of this plot showing that as the complexity

00:16:26.589 --> 00:16:31.770
of your model increases, the R squared will
increase and the training error will decrease.

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:36.850
But the really critical thing here is that
the test error is not going to continue to

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:40.220
decrease as you make your model more and more
complex.

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:45.740
Actually the test error is going to bottom
out for some certain level of model complexity,

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:48.480
which is indicated here using a red arrow.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.630
And after it bottoms out, it's going to start
increasing.

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.339
So you can see that in terms of test error,
you don't just want to fit an arbitrarily

00:16:55.339 --> 00:16:57.530
complex model.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:01.220
In order to make the test error small, there's
going to be some intermediate level of model

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.000
complexity.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:10.419
And actually if you take one thing out of
this webinar, it should be this figure.

00:17:10.419 --> 00:17:14.839
It should be an understanding of the fact
that the only thing that matters is test error.

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:17.620
Training error is just not relevant.

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:23.319
And if you focus on fitting your supervised
machine learning models in order to make the

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:27.249
training error small, then you've just totally
missed the boat, because you'll end up over

00:17:27.249 --> 00:17:36.270
here with a tiny training error but a really
substantial test error.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:42.610
So I just want to try to illustrate the problem
with thinking about training error in a small

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:43.610
example.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:49.330
So what I'm showing here on the left is just
a small simulated dataset with a single feature,

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:55.700
x, on the horizontal axis and a single response,
y, on the vertical axis.

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:57.659
And the observations are shown in red.

00:17:57.659 --> 00:18:03.720
And on the left is what you get if you just
fit a least squares linear model to the data.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:08.389
So in this example, there is like 20 observations,
one feature.

00:18:08.389 --> 00:18:13.789
I fit a linear model to the data using least
squares.

00:18:13.789 --> 00:18:19.190
And what I can see is that there's clearly
like a nonzero training error here, because

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:22.629
these red points aren't exactly on the black
least squares line.

00:18:22.629 --> 00:18:23.940
So the training error is nonzero.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.360
But this looks like a pretty good model.

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:33.889
I can imagine that if I had a test set, like
if I were able to generate more observations

00:18:33.889 --> 00:18:38.830
from the same distribution that made the red
observations, the test error would be pretty

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:39.830
modest.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:42.230
This is a pretty decent fit to the data.

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:44.450
But now in contrast, let's look over here
on the right.

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:47.100
So on the right, it's the exact same setup.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:52.580
But now instead of having 20 observations
like we had on the left, we just have two

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.119
observations on the right.

00:18:55.119 --> 00:18:59.499
And I'm, again, just fitting a linear model,
just with one variable, x.

00:18:59.499 --> 00:19:02.500
But what I see now is that I got a perfect
fit to this training data.

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:06.190
So my training error on the right is 0.

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:12.440
So we look at this picture on the right and
we see that I got 0 training error.

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:15.090
But this model on the right, it does not sit
well.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.080
This model doesn't look good.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:27.420
And if we imagine generating more observations
from the same distribution, we'd see this

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:28.779
actually is a really bad model.

00:19:28.779 --> 00:19:33.480
Those additional observations would actually
be nowhere close to this black line, because

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.049
we overfit the data here.

00:19:35.049 --> 00:19:39.029
We fit a model that was too complex given
the amount of data.

00:19:39.029 --> 00:19:43.870
With only two observations, you actually can't
fit a linear model in a meaningful way.

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:48.299
So over here on the right, we got 0 training
error, but the test error would actually be

00:19:48.299 --> 00:19:49.480
a huge.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:56.539
So this is kind of just an intuitive explanation
of the fact that training error is not the

00:19:56.539 --> 00:20:00.480
right thing to be looking at, because like
on the right here, the training error is 0

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.950
but we've got a really big test error.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:07.919
And this is also an example on the right of
the fact that even something that seems really

00:20:07.919 --> 00:20:13.590
simple like a least squares linear model can
actually be too complex for the data, and

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:18.730
in a little example with just two observations.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:23.940
So one thing that I just keep on referring
to offhand is this notion of model complexity.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:30.539
Model complexity is actually central to absolutely
everything in supervised learning.

00:20:30.539 --> 00:20:34.809
As model complexity increases, training error
will decrease.

00:20:34.809 --> 00:20:40.961
And I've kind of offhand been talking about
how we can increase the number of variables

00:20:40.961 --> 00:20:44.100
in our model and then the model will get more
complex.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:48.299
But actually, the notion of model complexity
is much more general than just the number

00:20:48.299 --> 00:20:50.330
of variables in the model.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:55.489
And we're actually going to see in a few minutes
when we look at rigid regression an example

00:20:55.489 --> 00:20:58.269
of a model that might include a lot of variables.

00:20:58.269 --> 00:21:00.640
But it's not such a complex model.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:05.119
So in other words, if it's convenient for
you to think about model complexity as like

00:21:05.119 --> 00:21:10.080
the number of variables in the model, then
you should just remember that that's one way

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:11.389
you can think about model complexity.

00:21:11.389 --> 00:21:14.320
But it's often not the best way.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:18.419
And model complexity actually is more general
than just the number of variables in your

00:21:18.419 --> 00:21:19.789
model.

00:21:19.789 --> 00:21:21.159
OK.

00:21:21.159 --> 00:21:24.590
So as model complexity increases, training
error will decrease.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:26.210
But the test error might not.

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:30.309
I already said this one.

00:21:30.309 --> 00:21:31.309
OK.

00:21:31.309 --> 00:21:35.730
So just to explain in a little more detail
what's happening in terms of the model complexity,

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:40.330
there's really two things we need to think
about when we think about how good a model

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:41.330
is.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:43.010
So the first thing is bias.

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:44.409
So the bias is beta hat.

00:21:44.409 --> 00:21:49.149
It's the average difference between beta and
beta hat if we were to repeat the experiment

00:21:49.149 --> 00:21:51.450
a huge number of times.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:58.220
So bias is basically telling us, how good
is our model on average?

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:03.630
And as we said, in increasingly complex model,
the bias is going to decrease.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:07.690
So this is really good, because I was just
saying that as we get a more complex model,

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:11.629
on average, our model is going to be better
and better and better.

00:22:11.629 --> 00:22:13.710
And that's great, because I want a really
good model.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:18.230
So that suggests that I should fit a really
complex model.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:21.460
But there's a problem, which is that it turns
out I don't just care about bias.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:25.960
I also care about something called variance.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.679
And the variance of beta hat is the amount
by which the beta hats will differ across

00:22:29.679 --> 00:22:30.679
the experiments.

00:22:30.679 --> 00:22:36.289
So imagine that I repeated an experiment a
huge number of times.

00:22:36.289 --> 00:22:39.470
And I calculated beta hat every time.

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:44.389
So the variance of beta hat is just the amount
by which these estimates of beta would vary

00:22:44.389 --> 00:22:47.270
across the experiments.

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:52.710
And the problem is that if I fit an increasingly
complex model, the variance of beta hat is

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:55.419
going to increase.

00:22:55.419 --> 00:23:00.919
And the test error, which is the thing that
I really care about, is actually the square

00:23:00.919 --> 00:23:03.970
of the bias plus the variance.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:08.559
So the thing that happens here is that there's
a trade off between bias and variance, because

00:23:08.559 --> 00:23:13.369
as I increase the model complexity, the squared
bias is going to decrease.

00:23:13.369 --> 00:23:15.129
So that's really good.

00:23:15.129 --> 00:23:16.860
But the variance is going to increase.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:19.639
And that's really bad.

00:23:19.639 --> 00:23:24.429
And so typically, if your model complexity
is really, really, really low, your bias is

00:23:24.429 --> 00:23:25.950
going to be huge.

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:27.980
And your variance is going to be small.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:32.200
But the sum of something huge and the sum
of something small is still huge.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.450
So that's bad.

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:39.460
And if you fit a super complex model, the
bias is going to be really small.

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:41.769
But the variance will be huge.

00:23:41.769 --> 00:23:46.519
And again, the sum of something small plus
huge is really huge.

00:23:46.519 --> 00:23:50.710
And typically, for some intermediate level
of model complexity, you can make both of

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:54.029
these relatively small simultaneously.

00:23:54.029 --> 00:23:56.070
And that'll make your test error small.

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:58.369
And that's the sweet spot.

00:23:58.369 --> 00:24:03.009
So what we want is a level of model complexity
that makes this sum, and consequently the

00:24:03.009 --> 00:24:06.620
test error, small.

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:12.529
So in other words, we need a model with small
bias and also small variance.

00:24:12.529 --> 00:24:15.899
So you can see this bias-variance trade off
in a picture.

00:24:15.899 --> 00:24:19.250
Model complexity from low to high is on the
x-axis.

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.419
Error is on the y-axis.

00:24:21.419 --> 00:24:25.269
You can see training error in blue, test error
in red.

00:24:25.269 --> 00:24:30.129
So you can see the training error is monotone
decreasing in model complexity.

00:24:30.129 --> 00:24:31.590
But the test error is not.

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:37.980
The smallest test error is happening right
here for an intermediate level of model complexity.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:42.840
And this figure also shows you that as you
get a more complex model, the bias is decreasing.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:45.030
But the variance is increasing.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:54.179
And for a less complex model, the bias is
increasing and the variance is decreasing.

00:24:54.179 --> 00:24:59.769
So hopefully by now you are on the same page
as me that test set error is the thing that

00:24:59.769 --> 00:25:02.299
we care about.

00:25:02.299 --> 00:25:07.149
So if we want to make sure that we're fitting
a model with a small test set error, then

00:25:07.149 --> 00:25:10.230
we need a way to estimate the test set error.

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:13.739
And basically there are three ways that we
can estimate the test error.

00:25:13.739 --> 00:25:15.139
And as you'll see, they're actually all pretty
similar.

00:25:15.139 --> 00:25:19.510
So the first one is the validation set approach.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:23.350
And the idea behind the validation set approach
is that you have your n observations.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:25.789
You split them into two sets.

00:25:25.789 --> 00:25:33.799
Train your model on one set and then calculate
the test error on the other set.

00:25:33.799 --> 00:25:35.980
So the validation set approach is great.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:38.230
It's very easy to implement.

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:43.169
The only problem is that you arbitrarily are
splitting your data into two sets.

00:25:43.169 --> 00:25:46.590
And if you had done a different split of the
data, you might get a slightly different estimate

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.590
of test error.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.679
People don't really like that.

00:25:50.679 --> 00:25:56.500
So to overcome that problem, we can do an
approach called leave-one-out cross-validation.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:02.480
And the idea behind leave-one-out cross-validation
is that we have our n observations.

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:05.809
We fit a model using all but the first observation.

00:26:05.809 --> 00:26:10.100
So you can see we're leaving out the first
observation in this top row here, fitting

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:14.580
a model on the remaining observations, and
then calculate the test error on the first

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.580
observation.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:21.590
Next we fit a model on all but the second
observation and evaluate the test error on

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:23.159
the second observation.

00:26:23.159 --> 00:26:24.210
And so on.

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:25.990
We do this n times.

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:29.720
Each time, we fit the model on all but one
of the observations.

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:32.580
And we test on the remaining observation.

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:35.269
Then we average the error rates that we get.

00:26:35.269 --> 00:26:39.759
And that gives us a really good estimate of
the test error.

00:26:39.759 --> 00:26:42.190
So leave-one-out cross-validation is really
nice.

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:43.710
It's very systematic.

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:48.370
It feels thorough, because we leave out every
observation one at a time.

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:55.759
But the problem is that often, it's computationally
undesirable, because we have to fit n models

00:26:55.759 --> 00:26:59.399
in order to get an estimate of our model complexity.

00:26:59.399 --> 00:27:04.070
And if n is a really big number, like we have
a really big sample size in our study, then

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.239
this isn't going to fly.

00:27:06.239 --> 00:27:11.679
So typically, the preferred approach is something
called k-fold cross-validation.

00:27:11.679 --> 00:27:15.159
And here I'm showing you an
example of k-fold cross-validation with k

00:27:15.159 --> 00:27:17.260
equals five.

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:21.409
And the idea is that we split the observations
into five sets.

00:27:21.409 --> 00:27:25.440
And we're repeatedly going to train the model
on four sets and evaluate its performance

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:26.980
on the fifth.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:31.649
So for example right here, in blue, you can
see my training set.

00:27:31.649 --> 00:27:36.019
I'm going to fit the model on the training
set, evaluate the test error on this test

00:27:36.019 --> 00:27:37.029
set in orange.

00:27:37.029 --> 00:27:39.049
And then I'm going to repeat.

00:27:39.049 --> 00:27:44.049
But I'm going to be changing each time which
set of observations I'm considering to be

00:27:44.049 --> 00:27:46.279
the test set.

00:27:46.279 --> 00:27:48.570
Average those five error rates.

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:52.440
And that gives us an estimate of our test
error.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:56.909
So it's really up to you how you want to estimate
test error.

00:27:56.909 --> 00:28:01.609
Leave-one-out cross-validation, the validation
set approach, and k-fold cross-validation

00:28:01.609 --> 00:28:04.090
are all perfectly fine.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:08.499
The important thing is that when you fit a
model, you need to check what its test error

00:28:08.499 --> 00:28:14.350
is, because you don't want to accidentally
come up with a model that has a great training

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:17.960
error but a very poor test error.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:20.149
OK.

00:28:20.149 --> 00:28:25.999
So far, we've talked about linear models that
we fit using least squares.

00:28:25.999 --> 00:28:33.019
But as I have alluded to, often least squares
regression is not a great choice of model.

00:28:33.019 --> 00:28:35.610
And this can basically happen for two reasons.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:39.979
It can happen because the least squares model
is not complex enough or because it's too

00:28:39.979 --> 00:28:42.309
complex.

00:28:42.309 --> 00:28:47.519
So I'm going to talk in a couple minutes about
what we do if a least squares model is not

00:28:47.519 --> 00:28:49.379
complex enough for our data.

00:28:49.379 --> 00:28:54.700
But actually typically, what we see in big
biomedical data is that the least squares

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:59.539
model is too complex in the sense that there
are too many features relative to the number

00:28:59.539 --> 00:29:02.210
of observations.

00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:06.909
So for example, suppose that I want to predict
a patient's response to chemotherapy.

00:29:06.909 --> 00:29:12.020
And my predictors are things like the expression
levels for a whole lot of genes, maybe for

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:19.669
20,000 genes, protein levels where I've assayed
some huge number of proteins, or maybe I've

00:29:19.669 --> 00:29:25.479
done DNA sequencing on genes that are potentially
implicated in breast cancer.

00:29:25.479 --> 00:29:31.149
So in each of these situations, chances are
we have a huge number of features or variables

00:29:31.149 --> 00:29:34.720
that we're using as predictors in our model.

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:38.360
And if we fit a least squares model in this
setting, we're actually going to get really

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:39.619
bad test error.

00:29:39.619 --> 00:29:43.970
Our training error would look awesome, but
the test error would be really bad, because

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:49.110
we'd be overfitting the data.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:52.860
So if we fit a model that's too complex, then
that's known as overfitting.

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:57.789
Basically a model that has a low training
error but a high test error is a model that

00:29:57.789 --> 00:29:59.539
we've overfit to the data.

00:29:59.539 --> 00:30:05.009
So typically, least squares is going to overfit
the data in the context of big biomedical

00:30:05.009 --> 00:30:06.340
data.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:09.029
And so what we need is
an alternative to least squares.

00:30:09.029 --> 00:30:14.029
We need a way to fit a linear model, but somehow
get less complexity than we get if we fit

00:30:14.029 --> 00:30:18.370
least squares using the P variables.

00:30:18.370 --> 00:30:24.720
So ridge regression and the lasso are two
methods for fitting a linear model that are

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.320
alternatives to least squares.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:32.830
And they yield a less complex model by shrinking
the regression coefficients.

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:37.669
And so as we're going to see, these are really
just minor modifications to the least squares

00:30:37.669 --> 00:30:38.889
fitting approach.

00:30:38.889 --> 00:30:46.139
But they can give us much better results in
cases when least squares fits too complex

00:30:46.139 --> 00:30:49.080
of a model.

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:54.979
So ridge regression and the lasso are examples
of what are known as regularization approaches

00:30:54.979 --> 00:30:59.010
or penalization approaches.

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:01.620
And there are many other examples of such
methods.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:06.119
But I'm just going to focus on ridge regression
and the lasso today, because those are the

00:31:06.119 --> 00:31:07.840
two best known approaches.

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:17.269
I also just want to mention that these regularization
approaches are actually a topic of extensive

00:31:17.269 --> 00:31:21.440
contemporary research in statistical machine
learning today.

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:22.769
OK.

00:31:22.769 --> 00:31:25.309
So first of all, ridge regression.

00:31:25.309 --> 00:31:30.479
The idea behind ridge regression is that we're
looking for coefficients, beta naught through

00:31:30.479 --> 00:31:34.889
beta P, that minimize our residual sum of
squares.

00:31:34.889 --> 00:31:41.860
So this is just like what we had in the least
squares setting.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:47.409
But also there's an additional term here,
which is a tuning parameter lambda times the

00:31:47.409 --> 00:31:52.609
sum over the features of beta j squared.

00:31:52.609 --> 00:31:58.679
So again, this is just like the least squares
optimization problem except that now there

00:31:58.679 --> 00:32:01.599
is an additional term.

00:32:01.599 --> 00:32:06.220
And so here, lambda is a non-negative tuning
parameter.

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:10.720
We can see right away that if lambda is 0,
then this term just disappears and we're just

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:14.539
fitting a least squares model.

00:32:14.539 --> 00:32:18.070
But as we increase lambda, this term is going
to tend to decrease.

00:32:18.070 --> 00:32:21.809
The sum of beta j hat squared is going to
decrease.

00:32:21.809 --> 00:32:25.929
And basically the way that you can think about
it is that there are two terms.

00:32:25.929 --> 00:32:31.320
This first term quantifies how well I'm fitting
the training data.

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:34.570
And this term quantifies how complex my model
is.

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:42.049
You can actually think of sum of beta j squared
as a way to quantify your model complexity.

00:32:42.049 --> 00:32:46.279
So when lambda is really small or when lambda
is 0, I'm just focusing on getting a really

00:32:46.279 --> 00:32:50.169
good fit to the training data with this lost
term.

00:32:50.169 --> 00:32:55.940
But when lambda is bigger, I'm going to be
focusing more on getting a really simple model

00:32:55.940 --> 00:33:00.809
or a model that's not too complex with this
penalty term.

00:33:00.809 --> 00:33:05.869
And actually in the limit, when lambda becomes
arbitrarily large, you can see that these

00:33:05.869 --> 00:33:13.509
beta j's are going to actually approach 0,
because if lambda is just super huge, then

00:33:13.509 --> 00:33:18.629
you're going to want your sum of beta j squares
to be really tiny so that this penalty term

00:33:18.629 --> 00:33:20.999
doesn't get too big.

00:33:20.999 --> 00:33:26.129
So you can see that lambda really controls
the complexity of your model.

00:33:26.129 --> 00:33:29.470
When lambda is really big, you're going to
get a really, really, really simple model

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:35.739
with just tiny coefficient estimates.

00:33:35.739 --> 00:33:41.779
So this plot gives you a graphical representation
of what ridge regression gives you as you

00:33:41.779 --> 00:33:44.250
vary the tuning parameter, lambda.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:45.929
So here I am showing lambda on the x-axis.

00:33:45.929 --> 00:33:49.039
The y-axis shows coefficients.

00:33:49.039 --> 00:33:55.220
And this is a little example data set with
a bunch of features, most of which are small.

00:33:55.220 --> 00:33:57.109
And those are shown in gray.

00:33:57.109 --> 00:34:01.620
And then four features that are larger are
shown in various colors.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:05.649
So in order to understand this figure, let's
take a look over here all the way on the left

00:34:05.649 --> 00:34:08.580
side where my cursor is.

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:11.660
So lambda is around 0.01 here.

00:34:11.660 --> 00:34:17.900
And when lambda is 0.01, like the black coefficient
is around negative 280, the red one is around

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:18.900
420.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:25.540
But as I increase lambda, for example as I
make lambda equal to one right here, the black

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:28.110
coefficient is slightly smaller.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.010
The red coefficient is slightly smaller.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:31.010
OK.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:32.429
These coefficients are slightly smaller.

00:34:32.429 --> 00:34:36.640
And as I make lambda really big, take a look
over here.

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:41.520
Like when lambda is 100, now all the coefficients
are quite a bit smaller.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:47.620
And over here where lambda is 10,000, all
the coefficients are really close to 0.

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:52.860
So when we perform ridge regression, our results
are actually a function of this tuning parameter,

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:53.930
lambda.

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:57.420
And if you want to know what would happen
with a given value of lambda, you just go

00:34:57.420 --> 00:34:58.860
to that position on the x-axis.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:05.030
And you look at the value of the coefficients
for that particular lambda.

00:35:05.030 --> 00:35:09.980
So over here, we're fitting a really simple
model with very low variance, because all

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:11.520
the coefficients are 0.

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:15.900
That's very low variance, but it's going to
have big bias.

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:19.000
So overall, the test error is going to be
really big.

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:23.480
Over here, probably the test error is going
to be big also, because this model has low

00:35:23.480 --> 00:35:26.320
bias but high variance over here.

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:30.570
And over here, things are more intermediate.

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:34.220
So probably the best value of the test error
is going to be around here.

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:38.630
But in order to find out, we'd have to use
one of the approaches that I mentioned earlier

00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:41.630
for estimating the test error.

00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:46.270
So one thing that I want you to notice is
that over here when lambda is really big,

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:48.500
all those coefficients become very close to
0.

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:51.090
But they're actually not exactly 0.

00:35:51.090 --> 00:35:53.440
Maybe they're like 10 to the negative three
or something.

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:54.770
We can't quite see in this figure.

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:57.590
But none of the coefficients are exactly 0.

00:35:57.590 --> 00:36:02.560
They're just becoming closer and closer to
0.

00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:06.020
So in practice, when you're performing ridge
regression, you're going to choose the tuning

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:10.670
parameter in order to minimize the test error.

00:36:10.670 --> 00:36:16.250
And then the value of lambda that we want
is the one with the smallest test error.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.970
And then
we would run ridge regression on the full

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:21.880
data using that same value of lambda.

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:28.860
So what I mean by this is like suppose you're
choosing lambda using the validation set approach.

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:37.211
So you fit the model on half of the observations
for a bunch of values of lambda, evaluate

00:36:37.211 --> 00:36:42.760
each of those models on the other half of
the observations, identify the value of lambda

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:45.360
that gave you the smallest error rate.

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:54.830
And now using that value of lambda, you need
to rerun ridge regression on all of the observations.

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:58.370
So the important thing to know about ridge
regression is that while it can give us a

00:36:58.370 --> 00:37:03.220
model that's not very complex when lambda
is large, that final model is going to contain

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:08.060
all of the variables no matter what.

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:10.980
And sometimes this isn't desirable.

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:15.150
So like imagine that you're trying to come
up with a way to use gene expression values

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.810
to predict a patient's survival time.

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:22.200
You might want to develop that model using
all 20,000 genes.

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:26.650
But at the end of the day, you might want
your final model to only involve a handful

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:31.910
of genes, because a final model involving
just a handful of genes would be easier to

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:34.080
translate into clinical practice, for example.

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:39.130
It would be less expensive to come up with
a diagnostic involving just 20 genes rather

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:42.340
than a diagnostic involving 20,000 genes.

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:46.430
So if we want to fit a model that involves
just a subset of the features, then ridge

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:49.680
regression isn't going to take us there.

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:52.040
So instead, we can do what's called the lasso.

00:37:52.040 --> 00:38:00.100
A lasso is just a small tweak to ridge regression
that gives us a model with mostly 0 elements.

00:38:00.100 --> 00:38:03.760
So in other words, the resulting model is
sparse.

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:12.200
And another way to phrase this is we can say
that the lasso performs feature selection.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:14.680
So this is the optimization problem for the
lasso.

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:18.460
We're going to look for coefficients that
minimize, once again, this residual sum of

00:38:18.460 --> 00:38:23.350
squares term that by now you've seen a lot
of times, plus a penalty.

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:27.430
This looks just like the ridge regression
penalty with just one difference.

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:30.600
In ridge regression, we had a beta j squared
term here.

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:35.210
Now I've got an absolute value of beta j.

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:37.070
And this is a really small difference.

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:39.310
And it seems like it shouldn't matter.

00:38:39.310 --> 00:38:44.240
But actually the difference is small, but
important, because whereas ridge regression

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:51.100
never gives you any coefficient estimates
exactly equal to 0, the lasso will.

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:56.510
So like ridge regression, we've got this tuning
parameter, lambda, that controls model complexity.

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:59.580
When lambda equals 0, we're just back at least
squares.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:05.030
But most importantly, for an intermediate
value of lambda, we can actually get some

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:08.840
coefficient estimates that are exactly equal
to 0.

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:13.220
And that's the whole reason to ever do the
lasso is if you want coefficient estimates

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:18.930
that are exactly 0-- in other words, if you
want a sparse model.

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:23.920
So when lambda is very large, the sum of the
beta j hats will be exactly 0.

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:28.640
In contrast to ridge regression, where the
best you'll ever see is the sum of the beta

00:39:28.640 --> 00:39:33.110
j hats might be close to 0.

00:39:33.110 --> 00:39:35.470
So we saw a figure like this for ridge regression.

00:39:35.470 --> 00:39:38.400
And now here's the same figure for the lasso.

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:42.530
And what I want you to notice here is for
example, check out what happens when lambda

00:39:42.530 --> 00:39:43.930
is 500.

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:48.110
So if lambda equals 500, the black coefficient
is nonzero.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:50.890
The yellow, red, and blue ones are nonzero.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:55.550
But all of these gray coefficients have estimates
that are exactly 0.

00:39:55.550 --> 00:40:01.030
So right here with lambda equals 500, my lasso
model only involves four features.

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:05.500
So this is really nice, because it means that,
again, if I'm trying to develop a predictor

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:12.120
of response to chemotherapy, my predictive
model here only involves four of my 20,000

00:40:12.120 --> 00:40:13.120
features.

00:40:13.120 --> 00:40:16.960
And that's pretty great in a lot of settings.

00:40:16.960 --> 00:40:21.460
If instead I had lambda equals 5,000, now
I have a fitted model that only involves the

00:40:21.460 --> 00:40:22.460
blue feature.

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:25.880
All the other features are exactly 0.

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:31.660
So again, as we saw with ridge regression,
I've got to choose this parameter, lambda.

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:37.500
The way that I typically would choose it would
be based on estimating the test error using

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:40.650
one of those approaches that we saw a few
minutes ago.

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:43.270
And that would tell us what value of lambda
to use.

00:40:43.270 --> 00:40:48.650
But for example, if that approach told us
to choose lambda over here, then our final

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:52.170
model would involve only four features.

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:54.200
OK.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:57.400
So that's it for ridge regression and the
lasso.

00:40:57.400 --> 00:41:03.610
I want to just briefly very fast mention another
approach for fitting a linear model that you

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:04.610
may have heard of.

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:07.370
And this is called principle components regression.

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:12.250
And it's really a different take on how we
can fit a less complex model than what we

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:14.860
get using least squares.

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:19.830
So the idea behind principal components regression
is as usual, we have n observations and P

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:21.060
features.

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:25.120
What that means is that our observations live
in a P dimensional space.

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:29.360
But it turns out that not all those dimensions
are equally useful.

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:34.010
So for example, some of the dimensions might
be completely redundant with each other.

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:38.380
And that would be the case if our features
are highly correlated.

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:40.830
Other dimensions might be uninformative.

00:41:40.830 --> 00:41:44.720
And that would correspond to noise features.

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:50.090
So the question is, can we find a low dimensional
representation of the variables that captures

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:52.200
most of the action?

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:58.420
So instead of having to deal with P dimensions,
can we actually just find n dimensions that

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:04.720
do a pretty good job of capturing what's happening
in the data?

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:09.640
So this is broadly referred to as a dimension
reduction approach.

00:42:09.640 --> 00:42:12.670
There are a lot of dimension reduction approaches
out there.

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:18.540
The two best known such approaches are principal
components regression and partial least squares.

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:22.780
And right now, I'm just going to mention principal
components regression.

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:28.390
But if you've heard of partial least squares,
then it's a similar type of approach.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:34.870
So the idea behind principal components regression
is that I'm going to look for the variables

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:37.600
that I'll call z1 through zn.

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.650
And these are linear combinations of my original
P predictors.

00:42:41.650 --> 00:42:46.770
So in other words, I'm looking for coefficients,
[INAUDIBLE] mj, such that zm is just a linear

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:50.550
combination of x's.

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:55.790
And then once I've identified these new dimensions,
z1 through zm, I'm just going to fit a least

00:42:55.790 --> 00:42:57.610
squares model.

00:42:57.610 --> 00:43:02.100
But instead of fitting the model using my
original features, I'm going to fit the model

00:43:02.100 --> 00:43:09.840
with my new features, z1 through zm.

00:43:09.840 --> 00:43:11.600
So this is really simple.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:14.750
I'm just telling you do least squares, but
don't use the original features.

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:16.120
Use new features.

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:17.120
OK.

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:19.360
Well what are these new features supposed
to be?

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:22.380
Well these new features should be the principal
components of the data.

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:27.550
So I'm telling you to take the first m principal
components of your data.

00:43:27.550 --> 00:43:30.160
Fit a least squares model using those as features.

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:31.160
And you're done.

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:33.520
You just did principal components regression.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:34.740
OK.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:36.620
So what are principal components?

00:43:36.620 --> 00:43:41.530
Well you're going to see these in more detail
next week in the webinar on unsupervised learning.

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:47.430
But the idea behind principal components is
that they're linear combinations of your variables

00:43:47.430 --> 00:43:51.810
that contain as much variability of your data
as possible.

00:43:51.810 --> 00:43:56.840
So they're basically just a low dimensional
representation of your data that captures

00:43:56.840 --> 00:44:00.150
as much of the variability as possible.

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:05.970
So in an example with two variables and observations
shown in purple, this green line represents

00:44:05.970 --> 00:44:07.880
the first principal component.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:12.240
And you can see the first principal component
is sort of the direction along which your

00:44:12.240 --> 00:44:14.901
data varies the most.

00:44:14.901 --> 00:44:18.710
So if I had to summarize these purple observations
in just one dimension, then this green line

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:23.720
would give me the best possible summary.

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:28.180
So the idea is when you do principal components
regression, you're actually getting a model

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:30.560
that's linear in the original predictors.

00:44:30.560 --> 00:44:33.870
So it is a linear model in the original predictors.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:38.540
But it's not the same as the least squares
model that you get if you ran these squares

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:40.390
on the original predictors.

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:46.870
You're actually getting a much less complex
model, provided that m is less than P.

00:44:46.870 --> 00:44:51.640
It's important to know that principal components
regression doesn't involve feature selection

00:44:51.640 --> 00:44:55.230
in the sense that all
of the original predictors are involved in

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:56.370
your final model.

00:44:56.370 --> 00:45:02.030
The number of principal components that you
have controls model complexity.

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:05.580
So if m equals P, you're just back at least
squares.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:10.240
If m is smaller than P, you're going to get
a less complex model.

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:14.830
And as usual, we're going to decide on the
level of level of model complexity by minimizing

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:16.880
the test error.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:18.470
OK.

00:45:18.470 --> 00:45:22.930
And then just as an aside, principal components
regression is actually very, very similar

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:30.210
to ridge regression in terms of the underlying
mathematics.

00:45:30.210 --> 00:45:33.070
And PCR works well in a lot of settings.

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:34.760
But sometimes it fails.

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:39.490
And really the reason for this is because
there's no guarantee that the first m principal

00:45:39.490 --> 00:45:45.310
components will be predictive of the response,
because the first m principal components will

00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:48.750
definitely explain a lot of the variation
in the features.

00:45:48.750 --> 00:45:52.820
But they might not be predictive of the response.

00:45:52.820 --> 00:45:54.930
OK.

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:59.950
So this whole discussion of ridge regression,
the lasso, and principal components regression

00:45:59.950 --> 00:46:05.070
have focused on giving you a model that's
less complex than least squares.

00:46:05.070 --> 00:46:10.950
And again, that's because in the case of biomedical
big data, usually least squares is too complex

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:15.700
of a model in the sense that you don't have
enough observations and you have a huge number

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:17.050
of features.

00:46:17.050 --> 00:46:22.240
But if it turns out that you're actually in
a setting where you want a more complex model,

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:27.890
like maybe you've just measured a dozen features
and you have 20,000 observations, then don't

00:46:27.890 --> 00:46:28.890
worry.

00:46:28.890 --> 00:46:33.490
Least squares has got you covered.

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:36.990
You can just fit a least squares model-- oops
I'm sorry.

00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:37.990
This is a typo.

00:46:37.990 --> 00:46:43.160
It should say we can fit a linear model using
transformations of your features.

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:50.170
So if you want to, you could perform least
squares using features xj, xj squared, xj

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:52.680
cubed, P to the xj.

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:58.500
You could even take products of your features
like xj with xk and so on.

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:01.110
And in order to fit the model, you could use
least squares.

00:47:01.110 --> 00:47:03.690
Or you could actually perform ridge, lasso,
and so on.

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:10.670
So it's always easy to make a model more complex
by just throwing in new features, or combinations,

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:13.680
or transformations of your old features.

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:18.640
And that's why I haven't really focused on
how to get a more complex model, because typically,

00:47:18.640 --> 00:47:20.380
that's a pretty easy task.

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:24.250
Just remember that if you're considering a
complex model, you've got to estimate your

00:47:24.250 --> 00:47:25.970
test error.

00:47:25.970 --> 00:47:27.390
OK.

00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:32.360
Briefly, I've been talking about regression.

00:47:32.360 --> 00:47:36.760
Classification is when you have a categorical
or a qualitative response.

00:47:36.760 --> 00:47:41.590
And the reason I haven't talked about classification
is because it's easy.

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:47.160
Everything that I've talked about for regression,
it applies exactly the same way to classification.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:51.550
So once you get it with regression, you're
going to understand classification too.

00:47:51.550 --> 00:47:54.270
All of the same ideas apply.

00:47:54.270 --> 00:47:55.270
OK.

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:57.780
Well I'm really almost out of time.

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:02.220
But I just want to spend two minutes talking
about a fun topic, which is decision trees.

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:06.020
So everything that I've mentioned so far is
within the framework of a linear model.

00:48:06.020 --> 00:48:10.810
But of course, there's other ways you can
do supervised learning too.

00:48:10.810 --> 00:48:13.750
And a really fun one is a decision tree.

00:48:13.750 --> 00:48:18.280
So the idea behind a decision tree is that
you are going to partition the range of x

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:19.880
into boxes.

00:48:19.880 --> 00:48:23.600
And then you're going to make a prediction
within each box.

00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:27.690
So decision tree models are very interpretable.

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:31.140
And to show you how interpretable it is, we
can look at an example.

00:48:31.140 --> 00:48:33.240
So this is just a toy dataset.

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:38.630
And what we're going to do is predict a baseball
player's salary based on various characteristics.

00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:43.950
So here is a regression tree for this baseball
dataset.

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:45.530
And so here is how you read it.

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.860
You start at the top and you say, all right.

00:48:48.860 --> 00:48:53.110
If this player's been playing baseball for
less than four and a half years, we're going

00:48:53.110 --> 00:48:54.450
to go to the left.

00:48:54.450 --> 00:48:57.130
And if they've been playing for more than
four and a half years, we're going to go to

00:48:57.130 --> 00:48:58.200
the right.

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:03.730
So whenever there is a branch or whenever
there's a split rather, if the answer is yes,

00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:04.850
you're going to go to the left.

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:06.730
And if it's no, you'll go to the right.

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:09.780
So let's say that there is a player who has
been playing for less than four and a half

00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:10.780
years.

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:11.780
OK.

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:12.780
We're going to go down to the left.

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:17.950
And if this player batted in fewer than 60
runs last season, we're going to go out to

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:18.950
the left again.

00:49:18.950 --> 00:49:21.570
If they had more than 82 put outs, we're going
to go to the right.

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:22.570
All right.

00:49:22.570 --> 00:49:23.570
Now we've got to check.

00:49:23.570 --> 00:49:28.350
Have they been playing for more then or fewer
than three and a half years?

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:31.710
And if they've been playing for more than
three and a half years, then my prediction

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:35.050
of their salary is 5.183.

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:39.260
So this is actually on the log scale and it's
in thousands of dollars.

00:49:39.260 --> 00:49:47.200
So to figure out how many dollars this is,
multiply or rather calculate e to the 5.183.

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:49.960
And then multiply by 1,000.

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:55.440
And the reason that this is on the log scale
is just because it's better to model salary

00:49:55.440 --> 00:49:58.180
on a log scale than on a linear scale.

00:49:58.180 --> 00:50:04.030
So alternatively, if the player who you want
to pick salary for has played for more than

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:11.630
four and a half years and they did more than
or 117.5 hits last season and fewer than 52.5

00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:17.910
walks, then they actually are going to have
substantially higher salary-- e to the 6.549

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:20.930
thousands of dollars per year.

00:50:20.930 --> 00:50:25.000
So regression trees are really great, because
you don't need to know any math.

00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:28.300
You don't need a computer to
understand them.

00:50:28.300 --> 00:50:33.870
Like imagine you're working in an emergency
room and there might just be some regression

00:50:33.870 --> 00:50:34.980
tree.

00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:40.180
And this can just be like pasted on the wall
of the nurse's station and it gives nurses

00:50:40.180 --> 00:50:45.760
a really quick way to just look up a patient's
characteristics and then get the prediction

00:50:45.760 --> 00:50:47.450
that they need.

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:49.260
So decision trees are interpretable.

00:50:49.260 --> 00:50:53.590
The whole reason to ever use a decision is
because you want a picture like this, because

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:59.080
you want something like this that you can
show to someone who either doesn't want to

00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:05.190
have to figure out all the math or just doesn't
have time and they need something quick that

00:51:05.190 --> 00:51:07.660
they can look at.

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:14.460
Regression trees typically are not as accurate
as other approaches to supervised learning.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:16.000
But they are interpretable.

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:18.110
So if you care about interpretability, then
great.

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:22.290
And if you don't care about interpretability,
this is not a good choice, because you can

00:51:22.290 --> 00:51:25.220
do better in terms of accuracy using other
methods.

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:31.990
So I just want to show in a little more detail
how do we get this tree.

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:36.540
Well suppose that our only variables were
the number of hits last season and the number

00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:38.990
of years that the player has been playing.

00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:44.340
So what we do is we partition feature space
in a very particular way in order to get these

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:47.440
three regions, R1, R3, and R2.

00:51:47.440 --> 00:51:51.570
And then to get the prediction within the
region, R1, we just take the average of the

00:51:51.570 --> 00:51:53.300
observations in this region.

00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:57.310
To get the prediction within R3, we average
those observations.

00:51:57.310 --> 00:52:02.630
And to get the prediction within this region,
we average those observations.

00:52:02.630 --> 00:52:07.810
And that gives us a tree that looks like this,
where the number reported here is the average

00:52:07.810 --> 00:52:13.040
within the regions, R1, R2, and R3.

00:52:13.040 --> 00:52:14.040
OK.

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:19.800
So when you're building a decision tree, you
can decide how big you want your tree to be.

00:52:19.800 --> 00:52:22.760
A large tree is going to tend to overfit the
data.

00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:26.830
A small tree is going to underfit the data.

00:52:26.830 --> 00:52:31.550
And we typically want to use the test error
in order to determine what's the best tree

00:52:31.550 --> 00:52:33.700
size.

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:38.440
So for example on the baseball data, on the
left we have a really big tree.

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:41.560
On the right, we have a much smaller tree.

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:44.610
The one on the left is going to have low bias
but high variance.

00:52:44.610 --> 00:52:49.170
The one on the right is going to have low
variance but possibly high bias.

00:52:49.170 --> 00:52:55.080
And the way that we would choose which tree
we want is by estimating the test error.

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:57.260
OK.

00:52:57.260 --> 00:53:02.880
So I just want to close with maybe one final
comment, which I don't have time to go into

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:03.880
detail.

00:53:03.880 --> 00:53:08.460
But this comment is basically that it doesn't
matter how fancy the machine learning is that

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:09.470
you're doing.

00:53:09.470 --> 00:53:13.750
You always have to think about just the underlying
science and the experimental design.

00:53:13.750 --> 00:53:19.670
And in particular, one really big problem
that we see within the context of big biomedical

00:53:19.670 --> 00:53:23.390
data is issues called batch effects.

00:53:23.390 --> 00:53:27.980
And batch effects can occur if there's some
kind of variability in your data that results

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:30.340
from nonbiological factors.

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:35.940
Batch effects are a huge problem in biological
data.

00:53:35.940 --> 00:53:39.280
And there's only so much that you can do in
order to deal with them.

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:46.380
In order to avoid having batch effects invalidate
your results, it's really important to have

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:48.230
good experimental design.

00:53:48.230 --> 00:53:51.000
So you should randomize your sample run times.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:55.580
For example, you don't want to run the cases
first and then the controls.

00:53:55.580 --> 00:54:00.770
You should try to avoid having batch effects
in the first place by reducing the extraneous

00:54:00.770 --> 00:54:03.900
sources of variation in your experiment.

00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:09.210
Typically, it's better if when you fit a model,
you fit the model on multiple data sets collected

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:15.060
in different labs or different institutions
rather than on a single dataset.

00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:19.710
And really most importantly, it's really important
that you validate any results that you get

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:23.640
on independent datasets collected in a different
lab or in a different institution.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:28.980
Because otherwise, the presence of batch effects
in your data could make your results look

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:29.980
really good.

00:54:29.980 --> 00:54:31.850
Even your test error could look really good.

00:54:31.850 --> 00:54:36.140
But if you had evaluated test error on an
independent dataset, it might not look so

00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:38.290
good.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:40.460
OK.

00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:44.240
And then finally, the last thing I want to
say is if you're doing supervised machine

00:54:44.240 --> 00:54:46.430
learning, there is no best approach.

00:54:46.430 --> 00:54:48.280
There's a lot of ways you can do it.

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:52.210
I've talked about a number of methods for
a supervised machine learning.

00:54:52.210 --> 00:54:58.000
But there's literally textbooks that are just
filled with supervised machine learning approaches.

00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:01.490
But what I want to emphasize is that there
is no best way.

00:55:01.490 --> 00:55:06.330
Sometimes one approach is going to work better
than another.

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:08.310
And so we can't say that one approach is best.

00:55:08.310 --> 00:55:11.060
But we can say that some approaches are wrong.

00:55:11.060 --> 00:55:15.910
So for example, you could do cross-validation
wrong.

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:17.870
And then your test error estimate would be
wrong.

00:55:17.870 --> 00:55:20.240
And you'd be choosing a really bad model.

00:55:20.240 --> 00:55:23.820
So no approach is best, but some approaches
are wrong.

00:55:23.820 --> 00:55:28.370
And if you get one thing from this lecture,
the thing that you get should be that test

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:32.550
error is really important, and that you've
got to make sure that you're calculating it

00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:33.550
right.

00:55:33.550 --> 00:55:39.290
Because a model that has a high test error
is just really meaningless and not useful

00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:40.930
actually in any context.

00:55:40.930 --> 00:55:41.930
Great.

00:55:41.930 --> 00:55:45.270
It looks like a lot of time.

00:55:45.270 --> 00:55:48.940
That's it, and I'm happy to answer questions.

00:55:48.940 --> 00:55:50.180
Thank you so much, Daniela.

00:55:50.180 --> 00:55:58.090
That was really a great overview of statistical
learning, supervised in contrast to what we'll

00:55:58.090 --> 00:55:59.290
hear about next week.

00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:04.181
I think one of the themes that ran through
your presentation was really how, with our

00:56:04.181 --> 00:56:07.470
modeling techniques, we struggle with that
curse of dimensionality.

00:56:07.470 --> 00:56:12.930
And we've had a series of questions basically
relating to this.

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:17.550
Is there an assumption with all the dimensionality
that we have and the complexity of these models

00:56:17.550 --> 00:56:22.350
that the underlying distributional properties
are normally distributed and Gaussian?

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:27.740
Or how much can we deviate from that and still
be able to get good estimates?

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:28.740
Yeah.

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:29.740
OK.

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:38.820
So Gaussianity is typically not a huge issue
in my opinion.

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:43.020
So like if you took your intro stats class
and you learned about linear models, you probably

00:56:43.020 --> 00:56:48.940
were told that you should assume that the
errors are normally distributed.

00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:52.040
And it turns out that's just not a huge assumption.

00:56:52.040 --> 00:56:54.850
It's not a big deal if your errors aren't
normal.

00:56:54.850 --> 00:56:58.680
You're probably fine as long as you have a
big sample size.

00:56:58.680 --> 00:57:02.670
But where you really run into trouble is when
your sample size is small relative to the

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:07.520
number of features you have or relative to
the amount of complexity of the model you're

00:57:07.520 --> 00:57:09.180
trying to fit.

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:14.450
And if your model is just too complex for
the available data, it doesn't matter what

00:57:14.450 --> 00:57:15.810
distribution your data has.

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:16.970
You're just in trouble.

00:57:16.970 --> 00:57:20.680
So the bottom line is that model complexity
is incredibly important.

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:24.590
In that question, you used the phrase curse
of dimensionality.

00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:29.510
And curse of dimensionality is just a very
evocative phrase to describe this phenomenon

00:57:29.510 --> 00:57:34.600
that I've mentioned, which is that a more
complex model, it can be a curse.

00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:39.460
Like if you measure more variables, don't
just assume that more data is better data,

00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:43.190
because more data just makes you tempted to
have a more complex model.

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:45.770
And that can just lead to a high test error.

00:57:45.770 --> 00:57:50.350
So the bottom line remains you just got to
think about test error.

00:57:50.350 --> 00:57:52.570
More complex is not better.

00:57:52.570 --> 00:57:54.660
Low test error is better.

00:57:54.660 --> 00:57:55.720
Right.

00:57:55.720 --> 00:58:01.370
So I think when a lot of people are building
their models, particularly using like a linear

00:58:01.370 --> 00:58:10.410
regression-based type of thing, and they look
at some sort of overall measure of fit or

00:58:10.410 --> 00:58:15.650
overall amount of variance accounted for,
for example, they typically want to look at

00:58:15.650 --> 00:58:18.770
the overall statistical significance of that
model.

00:58:18.770 --> 00:58:21.520
Like using an s statistic or something.

00:58:21.520 --> 00:58:26.620
And the degrees of freedom sort of capture
that model of complexity about those variables

00:58:26.620 --> 00:58:29.340
which are in the model versus those which
are part of the error term.

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:32.800
How does that act as a governor on those tests?

00:58:32.800 --> 00:58:38.900
And if that's not still acceptable, are there
alternatives like information-based metrics

00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:44.670
that one could use as a measure of overall
fit that may be more appropriate?

00:58:44.670 --> 00:58:45.670
OK.

00:58:45.670 --> 00:58:46.840
That's a great question.

00:58:46.840 --> 00:58:48.550
So there are a couple parts to that.

00:58:48.550 --> 00:58:56.110
So first of all, if you want to know how complex
your model is, however you phrase the question,

00:58:56.110 --> 00:59:02.180
I'm always going to give you the same answer,
which is the test error is the best way to

00:59:02.180 --> 00:59:06.150
figure out how well your model fits the data
and how complex it is.

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:09.700
Because there's all kinds of papers about
different methods, information, theoretic

00:59:09.700 --> 00:59:11.370
methods, and so on.

00:59:11.370 --> 00:59:15.700
But at the end of the day, like if I'm fitting
a predictive model, what I want is a model

00:59:15.700 --> 00:59:18.190
that fits a test observation well.

00:59:18.190 --> 00:59:19.190
Done.

00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:21.170
There's literally nothing else that I care
about.

00:59:21.170 --> 00:59:22.860
So test error is simple.

00:59:22.860 --> 00:59:23.860
It's intuitive.

00:59:23.860 --> 00:59:26.710
And it gets at what you want.

00:59:26.710 --> 00:59:31.260
Really I can't think of any example in a supervised
learning context where you'd care about anything

00:59:31.260 --> 00:59:33.310
else other than test error.

00:59:33.310 --> 00:59:38.200
Now another question that was part of that
was, how can we actually quantify model complexity?

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:40.660
[INAUDIBLE] degrees of freedom.

00:59:40.660 --> 00:59:45.620
And as you know, if you fit a linear model
using least squares, you can actually just

00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:53.710
quantify model complexity by just counting
the number of variables in your model.

00:59:53.710 --> 00:59:56.840
So you fit a least squares model with 10 variables.

00:59:56.840 --> 00:59:59.810
You used up 10 degrees of freedom.

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:00.810
Done.

01:00:00.810 --> 01:00:05.200
Unfortunately, as soon as you move away from
least squares to ridge regression, the lasso,

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:10.310
principle components regression, decision
trees, you can no longer just count up the

01:00:10.310 --> 01:00:12.190
number of variables that you're using.

01:00:12.190 --> 01:00:16.330
So a really good way to see that is in the
context of ridge regression.

01:00:16.330 --> 01:00:22.770
Like ridge regression, no matter what value
of the tuning parameter you have, you're always

01:00:22.770 --> 01:00:25.030
going to be using all P variables.

01:00:25.030 --> 01:00:29.220
So clearly, just counting the number of variables
in your model is not a good way to quantify

01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:31.030
model complexity.

01:00:31.030 --> 01:00:35.720
So model complexity, if you want to just say
like what's the complexity of model A versus

01:00:35.720 --> 01:00:40.840
model B where one is like a decision tree
and one is a lasso model, that's actually

01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:43.850
really a challenging thing to quantify.

01:00:43.850 --> 01:00:48.810
And I wouldn't typically recommend just trying
to go head to head between two models and

01:00:48.810 --> 01:00:50.840
figuring out which is more or less complex.

01:00:50.840 --> 01:00:54.000
Instead, I would, again, go back to test error.

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:58.290
And I would just choose the model with the
lowest test error, because test error is a

01:00:58.290 --> 01:01:03.690
universal quantity you can compare across
models of very different types.

01:01:03.690 --> 01:01:04.690
Great.

01:01:04.690 --> 01:01:06.660
Well we've run out of time, unfortunately.

01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:12.520
I'd like to encourage everybody who does have
questions for Dr. Witten, I'm sure that you

01:01:12.520 --> 01:01:14.100
can find her email address.

01:01:14.100 --> 01:01:18.530
It was on the kind of front of her slide set
there.

01:01:18.530 --> 01:01:22.440
And if you were to write to her with any questions,
I'm sure she'd be delighted to answer them.

01:01:22.440 --> 01:01:28.990
Once again, thank you, Dr. Witten, for giving
a great lecture on supervised statistical

01:01:28.990 --> 01:01:29.990
learning.

01:01:29.990 --> 01:01:35.250
Very appropriate to big data in a lot of contexts
for our listeners.

01:01:35.250 --> 01:01:36.710
And once again, thank you.

01:01:36.710 --> 01:01:37.880
And thank you, everybody.

01:01:37.880 --> 01:01:40.380
And we'll see you again next week.

01:01:40.380 --> 01:01:45.059
Thank you.

